{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KP6GRrtR1sohuMn8QJoQLd","type":"tool_result","content":"  1413→## Milestone 3 — nitrogen + oxygen cycle\n  1414→Deliver:\n  1415→- detritus pools\n  1416→- decomposition\n  1417→- nitrification guilds\n  1418→- oxygen dynamics\n  1419→- NH3 fraction calculation\n  1420→- event warnings\n  1421→\n  1422→## Milestone 4 — substrate + plants + algae\n  1423→Deliver:\n  1424→- substrate layers\n  1425→- plant guilds\n  1426→- algae/periphyton\n  1427→- light schedule\n  1428→- rooted vs water-column nutrient access\n  1429→- trim action\n  1430→\n  1431→## Milestone 5 — shrimp + microfauna\n  1432→Deliver:\n  1433→- shrimp population model\n  1434→- food-web linkage to biofilm/periphyton\n  1435→- reproduction / molt stress\n  1436→- high-temperature reproduction penalty\n  1437→- microfauna abstraction\n  1438→\n  1439→## Milestone 6 — TUI MVP\n  1440→Deliver:\n  1441→- overview/chemistry/biology/actions/log screens\n  1442→- time controls\n  1443→- scenario selection\n  1444→- save/load support\n  1445→\n  1446→## Milestone 7 — validation + balancing\n  1447→Deliver:\n  1448→- regression scenarios\n  1449→- parameter audit with provenance\n  1450→- balancing pass for plausible trajectories\n  1451→- documentation of known simplifications\n  1452→\n  1453→---\n  1454→\n  1455→## 22. Initial parameter packs required\n  1456→\n  1457→The first data pack must include:\n  1458→\n  1459→### Source water profile presets\n  1460→- soft acidic\n  1461→- moderate community-tank water\n  1462→- hard alkaline shrimp water\n  1463→- RO-like low-mineral water\n  1464→\n  1465→### Substrate presets\n  1466→- inert sand\n  1467→- inert gravel\n  1468→- active planted substrate\n  1469→- coarse porous media mix\n  1470→\n  1471→### Plant guild presets\n  1472→- fast stem\n  1473→- rooted rosette\n  1474→- moss/epiphyte\n  1475→- floating plant (optional v1.1)\n  1476→\n  1477→### Shrimp pack\n  1478→- `Neocaridina davidi`\n  1479→  - moderate temperature optimum\n  1480→  - high-temp reproductive penalty\n  1481→  - biofilm grazing preference\n  1482→  - GH dependence proxy\n  1483→  - moderate juvenile sensitivity\n  1484→\n  1485→---\n  1486→\n  1487→## 23. Known simplifications allowed in v1\n  1488→\n  1489→These simplifications are acceptable if documented:\n  1490→\n  1491→- aggregated microfauna guilds instead of species\n  1492→- approximate carbonate chemistry rather than full equilibrium across all acid/base systems\n  1493→- no explicit bacterial taxonomy beyond guilds\n  1494→- no spatial CFD\n  1495→- one well-mixed water column compartment\n  1496→- substrate represented as 1–3 layers rather than continuous depth\n  1497→- simple sex-ratio proxy for shrimp rather than individual mating model\n  1498→\n  1499→These are acceptable because the target is realistic aquarium dynamics, not full ecological omniscience.\n  1500→\n  1501→---\n  1502→\n  1503→## 24. Explicit non-negotiables\n  1504→\n  1505→The following must not be cut from the MVP:\n  1506→\n  1507→1. **different tank sizes**\n  1508→2. **ambient temperature as a real action**\n  1509→3. **temperature-dependent water behavior**\n  1510→4. **pH/temperature-dependent ammonia risk**\n  1511→5. **non-instant biological cycling**\n  1512→6. **substrate effects**\n  1513→7. **plant vs algae competition**\n  1514→8. **shrimp reproduction and failure states**\n  1515→9. **deterministic save/load**\n  1516→10. **text UI**\n  1517→\n  1518→If any simplification is necessary, simplify detail, not these core loops.\n  1519→\n  1520→---\n  1521→\n  1522→## 25. Research anchors for calibration\n  1523→\n  1524→These are not exhaustive; they are the initial anchor points the simulation should respect during calibration.\n  1525→\n  1526→1. Ammonia speciation and toxicity depend strongly on pH and temperature.\n  1527→2. Home aquarium biofilters can cycle at different speeds; in one recent study, ammonia and nitrite fell to undetectable levels by week 3 in two aquariums and by week 8 in another.\n  1528→3. Freshwater aquarium biofilters commonly contain AOA and comammox `Nitrospira`.\n  1529→4. Rooted submerged macrophytes can draw nutrients from sediment and/or the water column.\n  1530→5. Rising temperature interacts with nutrient regime and can shift aquatic plant performance and algal competition.\n  1531→6. Protozoan grazing changes bacterial biomass/community dynamics and nutrient turnover.\n  1532→7. `Neocaridina davidi` predates on meiofauna and can reshape those communities.\n  1533→8. `Neocaridina davidi` reproduction is inhibited at sufficiently high sustained temperature.\n  1534→\n  1535→These anchors should inform calibration and regression scenarios.\n  1536→\n  1537→---\n  1538→\n  1539→## 26. Example first sprint ticket list\n  1540→\n  1541→1. Define all core structs and enums.\n  1542→2. Implement geometry derivation and unit tests.\n  1543→3. Implement source water profile loader.\n  1544→4. Implement water change mixing.\n  1545→5. Implement ambient temperature and heater tick.\n  1546→6. Implement DO saturation utility.\n  1547→7. Implement TAN/NH3 helper.\n  1548→8. Implement detritus -> TAN pipeline.\n  1549→9. Implement nitrifier guild growth and oxidation.\n  1550→10. Implement daily plant update.\n  1551→11. Implement algae/periphyton update.\n  1552→12. Implement shrimp status model and daily reproduction check.\n  1553→13. Implement event emission layer.\n  1554→14. Build TUI overview screen.\n  1555→15. Add regression scenarios for nano vs medium tank.\n  1556→\n  1557→---\n  1558→\n  1559→## 27. Definition of done for v1\n  1560→\n  1561→v1 is complete when a user can:\n  1562→\n  1563→- start a shrimp-planted tank at one of several sizes\n  1564→- watch it cycle and mature over weeks\n  1565→- feed it, change water, and alter ambient temperature\n  1566→- see chemistry and biological state evolve over time\n  1567→- cause both healthy stabilization and realistic failure modes\n  1568→- observe shrimp breeding success or failure under changing conditions\n  1569→- inspect a log that explains why those outcomes happened\n  1570→\n  1571→That is the minimum successful product.\n  1572→\n  1573→\n  1574→---\n  1575→\n  1576→## 28. Reference set used for scientific anchoring\n  1577→\n  1578→These are the initial sources used to shape the first spec and calibration targets.\n  1579→\n  1580→- EPA CADDIS: **Ammonia** — ammonia occurs as NH3 + NH4+, higher pH favors the more toxic unionized NH3, and temperature also affects toxicity.\n  1581→- McKnight et al. 2025, **Microbial community succession of home aquarium biofilters associated with early establishment of comammox Nitrospira** — reported variable early-cycle trajectories in home aquarium biofilters, with undetectable ammonia/nitrite by week 3 in two aquariums and by week 8 in another.\n  1582→- McKnight et al. 2024, **Comammox Nitrospira among dominant ammonia oxidizers within aquarium biofilter microbial communities** — supports explicit treatment of comammox in freshwater aquarium biofilters.\n  1583→- Clarke 2001, **Sediment nutrient characteristics and aquatic macrophytes in lowland English rivers** — rooted submerged macrophytes can use nutrients from sediment and/or water column.\n  1584→- Zhang et al. 2020, **Interactive Effects of Rising Temperature and Nutrient Enrichment on Aquatic Plant Growth, Stoichiometry, and Palatability** — temperature and nutrient regime interact to change plant growth; external nutrient loading can suppress plants through algal competition.\n  1585→- Rønn et al. 2002, **Impact of Protozoan Grazing on Bacterial Community Structure** — protozoan grazing alters bacterial biomass/community structure and nutrient turnover.\n  1586→- Weber & Traunspurger 2016, **Influence of the ornamental red cherry shrimp Neocaridina davidi on freshwater meiofaunal assemblages** — shrimp predation can depress meiofaunal abundance, biomass, and secondary production.\n  1587→- Baliña et al. 2018, **Losing Reproduction: Effect of High Temperature on Female Biochemical Composition and Egg Quality in Neocaridina davidi** — high sustained temperature inhibited ovarian maturation and spawning at 33 °C relative to 28 °C.\n  1588→- USGS 2023, **Salinity and total dissolved solids measurements for natural waters** — supports treating TDS as a derived aggregate metric rather than the deepest internal state.\n  1589→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SdK2x77EeKtVykT9EyhLAo","session_id":"6255bc57-f0df-446f-b528-6846cbe58c9c","uuid":"57490820-f0b9-4d22-b0e4-c6817cae1f61"}
